Vault with HashiCorp Consul
Lab Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Install and configure HashiCorp Consul as a distributed key-value store
Set up HashiCorp Vault with Consul as the storage backend
Configure Consul for service discovery with Vault
Understand the integration between Vault and Consul for high availability
Perform basic operations with Vault using Consul backend
Verify service registration and discovery functionality
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Linux command line operations
Familiarity with JSON configuration files
Basic knowledge of distributed systems concepts
Understanding of key-value storage principles
Previous experience with HashiCorp Vault basics (recommended)
Lab Environment
Al Nafi provides Linux-based cloud machines for this lab. Simply click Start Lab to access your dedicated Linux machine. The provided machine is bare metal with no pre-installed tools, so you will install all required software during the lab exercises.

Task 1: Set up Vault with Consul as the Storage Backend
Subtask 1.1: Install Required Dependencies
First, update your system and install necessary packages:

sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
sudo apt install -y wget unzip curl jq net-tools
Subtask 1.2: Install HashiCorp Consul
Download and install the latest version of Consul:

# Create a directory for HashiCorp tools
mkdir -p ~/hashicorp
cd ~/hashicorp

# Download Consul
CONSUL_VERSION="1.17.0"
wget https://releases.hashicorp.com/consul/${CONSUL_VERSION}/consul_${CONSUL_VERSION}_linux_amd64.zip

# Extract and install Consul
unzip consul_${CONSUL_VERSION}_linux_amd64.zip
sudo mv consul /usr/local/bin/
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/consul

# Verify installation
consul version
Subtask 1.3: Create Consul Configuration
Create the necessary directories and configuration files for Consul:

# Create Consul directories
sudo mkdir -p /etc/consul.d
sudo mkdir -p /var/lib/consul
sudo mkdir -p /var/log/consul

# Create Consul user
sudo useradd --system --home /etc/consul.d --shell /bin/false consul
sudo chown -R consul:consul /etc/consul.d /var/lib/consul /var/log/consul
Create the main Consul configuration file:

sudo tee /etc/consul.d/consul.json > /dev/null <<EOF
{
  "datacenter": "dc1",
  "data_dir": "/var/lib/consul",
  "log_level": "INFO",
  "node_name": "consul-node-1",
  "bind_addr": "127.0.0.1",
  "client_addr": "127.0.0.1",
  "retry_join": ["127.0.0.1"],
  "server": true,
  "bootstrap_expect": 1,
  "ui_config": {
    "enabled": true
  },
  "connect": {
    "enabled": true
  },
  "ports": {
    "grpc": 8502
  }
}
EOF
Subtask 1.4: Create Consul Service
Create a systemd service file for Consul:

sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/consul.service > /dev/null <<EOF
[Unit]
Description=Consul
Documentation=https://www.consul.io/
Requires=network-online.target
After=network-online.target
ConditionFileNotEmpty=/etc/consul.d/consul.json

[Service]
Type=notify
User=consul
Group=consul
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/consul agent -config-dir=/etc/consul.d/
ExecReload=/bin/kill -HUP \$MAINPID
KillMode=process
Restart=on-failure
LimitNOFILE=65536

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF
Subtask 1.5: Start and Enable Consul Service
Start the Consul service and verify it's running:

# Reload systemd and start Consul
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable consul
sudo systemctl start consul

# Check service status
sudo systemctl status consul

# Verify Consul is running
consul members
consul catalog services
Subtask 1.6: Install HashiCorp Vault
Download and install Vault:

cd ~/hashicorp

# Download Vault
VAULT_VERSION="1.15.2"
wget https://releases.hashicorp.com/vault/${VAULT_VERSION}/vault_${VAULT_VERSION}_linux_amd64.zip

# Extract and install Vault
unzip vault_${VAULT_VERSION}_linux_amd64.zip
sudo mv vault /usr/local/bin/
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/vault

# Verify installation
vault version
Subtask 1.7: Configure Vault with Consul Backend
Create directories for Vault:

sudo mkdir -p /etc/vault.d
sudo mkdir -p /var/lib/vault
sudo mkdir -p /var/log/vault

# Create Vault user
sudo useradd --system --home /etc/vault.d --shell /bin/false vault
sudo chown -R vault:vault /etc/vault.d /var/lib/vault /var/log/vault
Create Vault configuration file with Consul as storage backend:

sudo tee /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl > /dev/null <<EOF
storage "consul" {
  address = "127.0.0.1:8500"
  path    = "vault/"
}

listener "tcp" {
  address     = "127.0.0.1:8200"
  tls_disable = 1
}

api_addr = "http://127.0.0.1:8200"
cluster_addr = "https://127.0.0.1:8201"
ui = true

# Disable mlock for development
disable_mlock = true
EOF
Subtask 1.8: Create Vault Service
Create a systemd service file for Vault:

sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/vault.service > /dev/null <<EOF
[Unit]
Description=HashiCorp Vault
Documentation=https://www.vaultproject.io/docs/
Requires=network-online.target
After=network-online.target
ConditionFileNotEmpty=/etc/vault.d/vault.hcl

[Service]
Type=notify
User=vault
Group=vault
ProtectSystem=full
ProtectHome=read-only
PrivateTmp=yes
PrivateDevices=yes
SecureBits=keep-caps
AmbientCapabilities=CAP_IPC_LOCK
Capabilities=CAP_IPC_LOCK+ep
CapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_SYSLOG CAP_IPC_LOCK
NoNewPrivileges=yes
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/vault server -config=/etc/vault.d/vault.hcl
ExecReload=/bin/kill --signal HUP \$MAINPID
KillMode=process
KillSignal=SIGINT
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5
TimeoutStopSec=30
StartLimitInterval=60
StartLimitBurst=3
LimitNOFILE=65536
LimitMEMLOCK=infinity

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF
Subtask 1.9: Start Vault Service
Start and enable the Vault service:

# Reload systemd and start Vault
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable vault
sudo systemctl start vault

# Check service status
sudo systemctl status vault

# Set Vault address environment variable
export VAULT_ADDR='http://127.0.0.1:8200'
echo 'export VAULT_ADDR="http://127.0.0.1:8200"' >> ~/.bashrc
Subtask 1.10: Initialize and Unseal Vault
Initialize Vault and save the keys:

# Initialize Vault
vault operator init -key-shares=5 -key-threshold=3 > ~/vault-keys.txt

# Display the keys (save these securely in production)
cat ~/vault-keys.txt

# Extract unseal keys and root token
UNSEAL_KEY_1=$(grep 'Unseal Key 1:' ~/vault-keys.txt | awk '{print $4}')
UNSEAL_KEY_2=$(grep 'Unseal Key 2:' ~/vault-keys.txt | awk '{print $4}')
UNSEAL_KEY_3=$(grep 'Unseal Key 3:' ~/vault-keys.txt | awk '{print $4}')
ROOT_TOKEN=$(grep 'Initial Root Token:' ~/vault-keys.txt | awk '{print $4}')

# Unseal Vault
vault operator unseal $UNSEAL_KEY_1
vault operator unseal $UNSEAL_KEY_2
vault operator unseal $UNSEAL_KEY_3

# Check Vault status
vault status
Subtask 1.11: Verify Consul Storage Integration
Login to Vault and verify the Consul integration:

# Login with root token
vault auth $ROOT_TOKEN

# Check Vault status
vault status

# Verify data is stored in Consul
consul kv get -recurse vault/

# Check Consul UI (if accessible)
echo "Consul UI available at: http://127.0.0.1:8500"
echo "Vault UI available at: http://127.0.0.1:8200"
Task 2: Use Consul for Service Discovery
Subtask 2.1: Register Vault Service in Consul
Create a service definition for Vault in Consul:

sudo tee /etc/consul.d/vault-service.json > /dev/null <<EOF
{
  "service": {
    "name": "vault",
    "tags": ["secure", "storage"],
    "address": "127.0.0.1",
    "port": 8200,
    "check": {
      "http": "http://127.0.0.1:8200/v1/sys/health",
      "interval": "10s"
    }
  }
}
EOF
Reload Consul to register the service:

# Reload Consul configuration
sudo systemctl reload consul

# Wait a moment for the service to register
sleep 5

# Verify service registration
consul catalog services
consul catalog nodes -service=vault
