Conclusion
In this lab, you have successfully:

Installed and configured HashiCorp Consul as a distributed key-value store and service discovery platform
Set up HashiCorp Vault with Consul as the storage backend, demonstrating how these tools integrate for enterprise-grade secret management
Configured service registration and discovery using Consul's service catalog and health checking capabilities
Implemented monitoring and health checks to ensure service availability and reliability
Created practical examples of service discovery clients that can dynamically locate and connect to services
Tested high availability scenarios including service failures and recovery procedures
This integration between Vault and Consul provides several key benefits:

High Availability: Consul's distributed architecture ensures Vault data is replicated and available even if individual nodes fail
Service Discovery: Applications can dynamically discover Vault instances without hardcoded addresses
Health Monitoring: Automatic health checks ensure only healthy Vault instances receive traffic
Scalability: The combination supports horizontal scaling of both storage and secret management capabilities
Understanding this integration is crucial for building resilient, production-ready infrastructure that can handle enterprise-scale secret management requirements. The skills you've developed in this lab form the foundation for implementing secure, distributed systems using HashiCorp's ecosystem of tools.
